---
source: tests/cli_config.rs
expression: text(&out.stdout)
---
Render a Markdown document, or show an image, in the terminal

Usage: kiri [OPTIONS] [FILE]...

Arguments:
  [FILE]...  Markdown file to render, a CSV or TSV file to show as a table, an image or a Mermaid
             diagram (.mmd) to show on its own, or a directory to show its README. Several files are
             shown one after another, each under a header that names it; one that cannot be shown is
             reported, and the others are still shown. Without one, or with -, kiri reads standard
             input unless it is a terminal; - can be given only once

Options:
      --doctor                 Show what kiri finds out about the terminal and which settings it
                               uses, as Markdown when written to a file, for a bug report
      --list-emojis            List the emoji shortcodes, such as :smile:. A terminal shows them as
                               a table; a pipe or a file gets one line per emoji
      --raw                    With --doctor: the report as Markdown also in a terminal, without its
                               sample, to copy into a bug report
      --format <FORMAT>        What the file is: markdown, csv, tsv, mermaid (a diagram shown on its
                               own), text (the file as it is, without rendering it), image, or an
                               image format such as svg, png or jpg. By default its extension and
                               content tell
      --width <WIDTH>          Width in cells; defaults to the terminal width
      --mode <MODE>            Which terminal features to use. Colours stay off when NO_COLOR is set
                               [default: auto] [possible values: auto, plain, ansi, kitty]
      --theme <THEME>          Theme file; overrides --light, --dark, KIRI_THEME and the
                               configuration file
      --light                  A light page in greys, whatever the terminal's colours; code colours
                               for a light background
      --dark                   A dark page in greys, whatever the terminal's colours; code colours
                               for a dark background
  -M, --no-margins             No margins, heading indents or code block padding, for copying text
  -I, --no-images              Show images as their alt text, without reading them, and diagrams as
                               code. An image or diagram file given on its own is still shown
      --images                 Show images, and diagrams unless they are off, whatever
                               KIRI_NO_IMAGES and the configuration file say
      --paging <WHEN>          When to show the document in the pager: always, auto (when it is
                               longer than the window and the output is a terminal) or never
                               [possible values: always, auto, never]
  -p                           Show the document in the pager: --paging=always
  -P                           Print the document without the pager: --paging=never
      --follow <WHEN>          Follow a lone file in the pager: on (read it again whenever it
                               changes, with the view kept where it is), auto (read it again and
                               move the view to a change) or off; off unless set. Following pages
                               the file, however short, unless paging is never. F goes from off to
                               on to auto in the pager [possible values: on, off, auto]
  -f                           Follow the file, in the pager: --follow=on
      --fa                     Follow the file and move to a change: --follow=auto
  -F                           Do not follow the file: --follow=off
      --follow-jump <WHICH>    Which change auto-follow moves the view to, when a reload changed the
                               document in more than one place: first, last or most (the one that
                               takes the most rows); first unless set [possible values: first, last,
                               most]
      --first-load <HOW>       Whether the pager shows the document once its images and diagrams
                               have loaded (wait), or at once, with each image as it comes
                               (background); wait unless set. Only where the pager surely opens, as
                               with -p or -f; otherwise kiri waits [possible values: wait,
                               background]
      --front-matter <STYLE>   Front matter render style: table, code (a code block), hide, or off
                               (read as Markdown); table unless set. Front matter is the metadata
                               block at the start of a Markdown file [possible values: table, code,
                               hide, off]
      --table-rows <STYLE>     Table row style: zebra (every second row shaded where the terminal's
                               colours are known), lines, indent (the lines after the first of a
                               wrapped value start two cells in), underline (each row's last line
                               underlined), or off; zebra unless set [possible values: off, zebra,
                               lines, indent, underline]
      --diagrams <SWITCH>      Diagrams in code blocks, such as Mermaid: on (drawn as images) or off
                               (shown as code); on unless set [possible values: on, off]
      --render-errors <WHERE>  Where to tell why a diagram could not be drawn: inline (in the
                               document, below its code), stderr (a warning) or both; both unless
                               set [possible values: inline, stderr, both]
      --max-image-size <MB>    Largest image file to read, in megabytes (at most 100); larger images
                               show their alt text [default: 10]
  -h, --help                   Print help (see more with '--help')
  -V, --version                Print version

Images and diagrams are drawn only in terminals that support kitty's graphics protocol with Unicode
placeholders, such as kitty 0.28 or later; elsewhere images show their alt text and diagrams their
code.
